# dungeon.zd — an accumulator that survives across iterations.
#
# Ported from `src/lib/game/dungeon.ts` in the portfolio this language is
# measured against: `emptyFloorTile` (line 84) and the placement loops in
# `generateFloor` that call it (lines 133-183).
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS EVIDENCE FOR. The gap analysis of 2026-08-03 (§3.4)
# recorded the dungeon generator as the one thing on its list with no
# substitute anywhere:
#
#   > It rejection-samples up to 400 times against a `Set` of positions
#   > already claimed by earlier placements, and that set is built up
#   > across the placements. There is no local binding, no accumulator,
#   > and `each`+`append` grows only a top-level signal and only inside
#   > an event handler — never inside a pure function. So enemies and
#   > items cannot be placed without collision.
#
# That was measured against a compiler with neither local bindings nor a
# tail-call rewrite. Both landed, and between them the sentence stopped
# being true: `taken` below is an ordinary parameter, it is threaded from
# one placement to the next by an ordinary self call, and the emitter
# turns that call into a jump so four hundred rejections cost four hundred
# iterations and one stack frame. **No new construct, and no new word.**
#
# What a pipeline still cannot do is carry a value from one of its
# iterations to the next, and neither can `each`. That remains true. It is
# also not what this program needed: the placement loop is a fold with
# state, and a fold with state is a function that calls itself.

# The board. 40x24 is `MAP_W` and `MAP_H`.
state width  is client Whole starting 40
state height is client Whole starting 24
state depth  is client Whole starting 3
state seed   is client Whole starting 20259

# The rooms `carveRoom` would have carved. An L-shaped corridor has a
# closed form and a room is a rectangle, so a tile does not have to be
# written into to know what it is.
# One physical line: a collection literal may not span lines.
state rooms is client List of Room starting [(Room with x is 2, y is 2, w is 8, h is 5), (Room with x is 14, y is 3, w is 9, h is 6), (Room with x is 28, y is 2, w is 8, h is 7), (Room with x is 4, y is 12, w is 9, h is 6), (Room with x is 18, y is 14, w is 10, h is 7), (Room with x is 31, y is 13, w is 6, h is 8)]

record Room
    x is Whole
    y is Whole
    w is Whole
    h is Whole

# One rejection-sampled tile, plus the two things that have to outlive it:
# where the generator's random stream got to, and what is now claimed.
record Spot
    index is Whole
    seed  is Whole
    taken is List of Whole

# The accumulator itself. `taken` is the `Set<number>` of `dungeon.ts:133`
# and `seed` is the `rng` closure's hidden state made honest.
record Placement
    seed   is Whole
    taken  is List of Whole
    spots  is List of Whole

# --- arithmetic the numeric prelude would otherwise supply ---------------
#
# `mod` and `randomBelow` live on a branch this one does not carry, so
# they are written out here rather than left as a dependency. A floored
# remainder over non-negative operands is all the generator asks for.

function modOf with value, divisor
    give value - ((floor of ((decimalOf of value) / (decimalOf of divisor))) * divisor)

function nextSeed of seed
    give modOf with value is (seed * 25173 + 13849), divisor is 65536

function randomBelow with seed, bound
    give modOf with value is seed, divisor is bound

# --- the map -------------------------------------------------------------

function roomHolds with room, x, y
    give (x >= room.x) and (x < room.x + room.w) and (y >= room.y) and (y < room.y + room.h)

# `state.tiles[i] === 'floor'`, asked of the rooms rather than of a grid
# that was written into.
function isFloorAt with rooms, x, y
    give isFloorFrom with rooms is rooms, x is x, y is y, position is 0

function isFloorFrom with rooms, x, y, position
    when listAt with value is rooms, index is position
        None
            give no
        Some with room
            if roomHolds with room is room, x is x, y is y
                give yes
            give isFloorFrom with rooms is rooms, x is x, y is y, position is position + 1

# The tile grid, built at `width * height` rather than at a length written
# into this file. `indices` is what makes that possible and `map each`
# binds the position because the position is the datum.
#
# COST. O(width * height): `indices` is linear and `map` preserves length.
function tilesOf with rooms, width, height
    from indices of (width * height)
    map each i to (glyphAt with rooms is rooms, index is i, width is width)

function glyphAt with rooms, index, width
    with y is (floor of ((decimalOf of index) / (decimalOf of width)))
    if isFloorAt with rooms is rooms, x is index - (y * width), y is y
        give "."
    give "#"

# --- the placement loop --------------------------------------------------

# `emptyFloorTile(state, rng, taken)`, dungeon.ts:84.
#
# Up to `tries` draws; the first that lands on floor nobody has claimed
# wins and *claims it*, so the answer carries a longer `taken` than it was
# given. Exhausting the tries falls back to the player's own tile, exactly
# as the original does.
#
# COST. O(tries) draws, each O(rooms) to classify the tile and O(taken) to
# reject it. Constant stack depth: the retry is a self call in tail
# position, so four hundred rejections are four hundred iterations.
function emptyFloorTile with rooms, seed, taken, tries, fallback
    if tries is 0
        give Spot with index is fallback, seed is seed, taken is taken
    with sx is (nextSeed of seed)
    with sy is (nextSeed of sx)
    with x is (randomBelow with seed is sx, bound is 40)
    with y is (randomBelow with seed is sy, bound is 24)
    with here is (y * 40 + x)
    if (isFloorAt with rooms is rooms, x is x, y is y) and (not (taken contains here))
        give Spot with index is here, seed is sy, taken is (append here to taken)
    give emptyFloorTile with rooms is rooms, seed is sy, taken is taken, tries is tries - 1, fallback is fallback

# **The loop the gap analysis said could not be written.** `place` goes in
# and a longer `place` comes out, once per entity, and the `taken` inside
# it is the same set the previous iteration added to.
#
# COST. O(n) iterations for n placements, each bounded by `emptyFloorTile`
# above. Constant stack depth, by the same tail position.
function placeAll with rooms, place, remaining, fallback
    if remaining < 1
        give place
    with spot is (emptyFloorTile with rooms is rooms, seed is place.seed, taken is place.taken, tries is 400, fallback is fallback)
    give placeAll with rooms is rooms, place is (Placement with seed is spot.seed, taken is spot.taken, spots is (append spot.index to place.spots)), remaining is remaining - 1, fallback is fallback

# `generateFloor`'s two placement passes, sharing one `taken` across both
# — which is the whole point of the reservation: free loot must not land
# on an enemy the earlier pass already put down.
function generateFloor with rooms, seed, depth, start
    with opening is (Placement with seed is seed, taken is (append start to empty), spots is empty)
    with enemies is (placeAll with rooms is rooms, place is opening, remaining is 3 + depth, fallback is start)
    give placeAll with rooms is rooms, place is enemies, remaining is 2 + depth, fallback is start

state level  is client Placement from generateFloor with rooms is rooms, seed is seed, depth is depth, start is 90
state tiles  is client List of Text from tilesOf with rooms is rooms, width is width, height is height
state placed is client Whole from length of level.spots
state claimed is client Whole from length of level.taken

view
    Column
        Heading "Dungeon"
        Text "entities placed"
        Text placed
        Text "tiles claimed, including the player's own"
        Text claimed
        Text "tiles in the map"
        Text (length of tiles)
